2025-11-01 20:46:19,509 - app.llm.services - INFO - Generating with model: llama3.1:70b, temperature: 0.3, max_tokens: 500, timeout: 60
2025-11-01 20:46:19,509 - app.llm.services - DEBUG - [REQUESTS] Sending request to Ollama: {'model': 'llama3.1:70b', 'prompt': 'You are analyzing news articles for a Scottish heritage and culture newsletter.\n\nArticle Title: Edinburgh schools: Take a look at campus plans for new \'state-of-the-art\' primary school in South Queensferry\nURL: https://www.scotsman.com/news/new-edinburgh-primary-school-south-queensferry-5383317\n\nArticle Content (first 2000 chars):\nPlans for a new primary school and nursery with capacity for over 500 children in South Queensferry have been submitted toEdinburgh City Council. Proposals for the new Builyeon Road Primary School and Nursery were lodged on October 7 for a two-stream primary school for 420 children and a nursery for 128 children on the land south of Builyeon Road in Echline. Advertisement Advertisement The school would be located between the two motorways leading to the Forth Road Bridge and theQueensferry Crossing. The area earmarked for hundreds of new homes, with housing construction already underway. Previous plans submitted said the new school could open in August 2027 at the earliest. The two-storey primary school is to become the fifth school in the town, joining Echline Primary, St Margaret\'s R C Primary, Queensferry Primary and Queensferry High School. Advertisement Advertisement The new school and nursery campus is set to include extensive sports facilities, including a two-court hall along with an external 2G artificial pitch of around 60m x 40m. These facilities will also be open to the wider community. The external plans also show two cycle stores for pupils with 42 spaces in total, outdoor dining space, accessible parking and a staff cycle store. As well as active play space with play structures, the main playground with cycle proficiency track, a woodland trail and outdoor teaching space. Also included in the plans for the new school is an outdoor classroom with space for a full class, with in-built seating and chalkboards, which would be a fully covered space for use in all weathers. Advertisement Advertisement The plans also include a growing space, with raised planters, an external tap, a poly-tunnel and orchard trees, while the informal open play space would be a grassed area for informal sports, free play and group activities. The proposed new school is part of the Builyeon Road Masterplan for the former farmland area south of South Queensferry, which was approve\n\nYour task:\n1. Evaluate the article\'s suitability for a Scottish heritage/culture newsletter (0-10 scale):\n   - 10: Highly relevant (Scottish heritage, culture, traditions, history, notable Scottish people/places/events)\n   - 7-9: Very relevant (Scottish topics, community stories, cultural events, regional news)\n   - 5-6: Moderately relevant (peripheral Scottish connection)\n   - 3-4: Somewhat relevant (weak connection)\n   - 0-2: Not relevant (no clear Scottish connection, purely local interest)\n\n2. Generate a brief synopsis (2-3 sentences) summarizing the key points relevant to Scottish heritage/culture.\n\n3. If the article is purely local interest without broader Scottish relevance, mark it as not relevant.\n\nProvide your assessment in JSON format:\n{\n    "score": <number 0-10>,\n    "reasoning": "<brief explanation of why this score, mention Scottish connections or why it\'s not suitable>",\n    "synopsis": "<2-3 sentence summary focusing on Scottish heritage/culture aspects, or \'Not relevant for newsletter\' if score < 6>",\n    "relevant": <true if score >= 6.0, false otherwise>\n}\n\nBe strict but fair. Only articles with genuine Scottish connections should score 6.0 or higher.\n', 'temperature': 0.3, 'max_tokens': 500, 'stream': False}
